# C++ and MFC (Envy)

## C++ baseline

- Target C++20; keep changes C++17-compatible unless the project is bumped.
- RAII ownership; raw pointers are non-owning unless documented.
- `nullptr`, `override`, `constexpr`, `noexcept` (not `throw()`).
- Prefer small local modernizations; avoid rewrite refactors.

## MFC hard rules

- Never block the UI thread on network/disk work.
- Never touch UI controls from worker threads — use `PostMessage`.
- Use `CCriticalSectionPtr`, `CQuickLock`, `CLocked<T>` for shared state.
- Prefer `CString`, `_T("...")`, Unicode paths in MFC-facing code.

## GUI resource de-duplication

Before adding dialog/control/menu/toolbar/icon/string resources:

1. Search by ID, symbolic name, and caption text.
2. Search code usage (`IDD_*`, `ID_*`, `DDX_*`, message maps).
3. Reuse or extend existing resources when possible.

If a new resource is unavoidable, document IDs checked, chosen slot, and usage site.

## Naming (actual codebase)

- Classes: `C` + PascalCase; members: `m_s`, `m_n`, `m_b` (`BOOL`), `m_p`, `m_h`, `m_t`, `m_o`
- Handlers: `On...`; files match primary class name

## Error handling

- Check Win32/MFC return values; include context in failures.
- `theApp.Message(MSG_ERROR, ...)` for user-visible errors.
- `ASSERT` for debug invariants; `VERIFY` when side effects must run in release.
- Treat malformed network input as protocol errors — reject safely.

## Performance

- Profile before optimizing; avoid allocations/logging in hot loops.
- Pre-size containers; prefer `const&` for large inputs.
- Batch UI updates for large lists.
